00:06:19.692 --> 00:06:21.650
So let's start off with
the Dataprep lifecycle.

00:06:21.650 --> 00:06:25.610
This is just a quick
diagram for that.

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:27.200
Starting off on the left here.

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:29.010
You're starting
off with raw data.

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:31.610
The goal is then to discover
what's inside of it,

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:35.480
so that you can structure
and clean the data, right?

00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:40.460
And then clean the data, remove
obvious data quality errors, so

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.010
that you can then go
to the enrich face,

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:45.380
where you're combining it,
joining it, aggregating--

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:47.780
basically transforming
the data to what you need.

00:06:47.780 --> 00:06:49.650
Following that,
then you'll validate

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:53.030
it to make sure it conforms
to your organization's rules

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:54.530
and definitions.

00:06:54.530 --> 00:06:58.093
And ideally, this whole thing
goes into an analytics--

00:06:58.093 --> 00:06:59.510
downstream analytics,
like I said,

00:06:59.510 --> 00:07:03.357
Data Studio or ML
initiative from there.

00:07:03.357 --> 00:07:05.690
Now, you might notice, this
is a very iterative process,

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:06.830
and that's intentional.

00:07:06.830 --> 00:07:09.375
So you can do the whole thing
through the Dataprep interface,

00:07:09.375 --> 00:07:11.000
where you're kind of
going through this

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:12.792
and cleaning your data,
preparing your data

00:07:12.792 --> 00:07:13.650
to the point where--

00:07:13.650 --> 00:07:15.900
to exactly what you need,
exactly with your standards.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:18.140
And the goal here is for
you to do this yourself.

00:07:18.140 --> 00:07:19.557
And you can do
this really rapidly

00:07:19.557 --> 00:07:22.500
without any additional help.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.040
Now, of course, once you get
that data into that format

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:27.110
that you need, you
can then automate it,

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:29.990
you can fully automate it,
and you can schedule it,

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:31.040
so it runs periodically.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:32.582
So now you've
empowered your pipeline

00:07:32.582 --> 00:07:35.300
to run without you being
there and just updating,

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:38.190
and your downstream systems.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:41.070
So that's the high level
Dataprep lifecycle.

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:43.280
Now let's get into the
challenges associated with it.

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:44.960
These ones are
specific scenarios

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:47.690
that we've seen with
customers in different phases

00:07:47.690 --> 00:07:49.890
along this journey.

00:07:49.890 --> 00:07:51.620
So let's start off
with the discovery

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:53.240
and structuring phase.

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:56.720
And while it's really nice when
the data is in perfect rows

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:59.060
and columns and no
issues up front,

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:02.030
very often you get
semi-structured data or even

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:03.710
unstructured data.

00:08:03.710 --> 00:08:06.085
And those things, in order
to get any value out of that,

00:08:06.085 --> 00:08:08.210
you actually need to access
the individual elements

00:08:08.210 --> 00:08:10.020
and dig into it from there.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:12.500
So that's the first type of
problem that we'll be tackling,

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:15.240
how do you handle that
kind of nested data.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.128
Following that, as part of the--

00:08:18.128 --> 00:08:19.920
after you've structured
the data and you're

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:21.810
going into the
cleaning phase, you'll

00:08:21.810 --> 00:08:24.007
often find that the
data doesn't necessarily

00:08:24.007 --> 00:08:26.090
have the right format for
what you're looking for.

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:28.305
Often it's actually a
slightly different pattern

00:08:28.305 --> 00:08:29.680
you're dealing
with in your data.

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:31.680
And again, it's
very hard to make

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:33.659
use of data that's all
of different formats,

00:08:33.659 --> 00:08:35.190
of different patterns
inside of it.

00:08:35.190 --> 00:08:36.359
You want to standardize
that, or you

00:08:36.359 --> 00:08:38.520
want to normalize that
into something standard,

00:08:38.520 --> 00:08:41.250
so that you can actually, again,
perform your transformations

00:08:41.250 --> 00:08:43.720
and cleanse it.

00:08:43.720 --> 00:08:45.520
And next is on
the cleaning side.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:48.070
While you can apply
common data quality rules,

00:08:48.070 --> 00:08:55.450
removing nulls, checking for
statistics against the data,

00:08:55.450 --> 00:08:57.970
you may also
encounter situations

00:08:57.970 --> 00:08:59.960
where humans have
entered the data,

00:08:59.960 --> 00:09:02.200
so there's issues with
the entry of the data

00:09:02.200 --> 00:09:04.022
that it just doesn't
fit any pattern.

00:09:04.022 --> 00:09:06.230
It's really hard to figure
out what those things are.

00:09:06.230 --> 00:09:08.655
So this is actually before
you start doing pivots

00:09:08.655 --> 00:09:10.780
and joins on things, you
want to actually make sure

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:13.960
that that data is cleaned
up and standardized.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:19.570
From there, what
we'll do next is then

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:21.850
talk about validation
of the data.

00:09:21.850 --> 00:09:24.130
So remember the
whole goal of this

00:09:24.130 --> 00:09:26.710
is to make sure that your
data is ready for analytics

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:28.307
or downstream modeling.

00:09:28.307 --> 00:09:30.640
But how do you know your
downstream system will actually

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:31.627
accept your results?

00:09:31.627 --> 00:09:33.460
All these systems have
very specific schemas

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:36.940
they're expecting, expecting
the data in a certain pattern

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:38.080
order.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:40.640
How do you ensure that that
is actually going to work?

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:42.080
And of course, if you
want to automate all this,

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.372
you have to make sure that
thing's working consistently

00:09:44.372 --> 00:09:45.770
and working each time.

00:09:45.770 --> 00:09:48.970
So these are the four challenges
we'll be talking about.

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:54.350
And we'll use the following
scenarios to talk through them.

00:09:54.350 --> 00:09:56.100
The first one is going
to be understanding

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:57.810
nested data, where
we'll show you

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:00.030
how to unpack and
extract information

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:02.380
out of deeply nested elements.

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:04.165
Next is normalizing
data patterns.

00:10:04.165 --> 00:10:06.540
We'll talk about profiling,
understanding those patterns,

00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:08.207
and then converting
them into a standard

00:10:08.207 --> 00:10:09.510
that you're looking for.

00:10:09.510 --> 00:10:11.430
When we talked about
inconsistent data,

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:13.590
we'll show you something
new with Dataprep

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:16.240
around clustering and
standardizing your data.

00:10:16.240 --> 00:10:17.947
And the last one,
validating results,

00:10:17.947 --> 00:10:20.280
we'll show you how to compare
and match against your end

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:22.590
target, so that you know
what you're generating

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:25.560
is actually going to be
successful in your end system.

00:10:28.600 --> 00:10:29.170
All right.

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:31.880
So let's get started.

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:34.918
I'll cover the first two,
nested data and data patterns.

00:10:34.918 --> 00:10:36.460
And then, like I
said, we'll actually

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:38.650
demonstrate some of these.

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:41.800
So thinking of nested data,
all right, often users

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:44.560
start off with
nested file formats.

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:47.567
So JSON, AVRO, XLSX, PQT.

00:10:47.567 --> 00:10:49.150
That's just the
starting part of that.

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:52.540
So let's take a look at
what this looks like.

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:56.080
I hope no one can read this,
because this is actually

00:10:56.080 --> 00:10:57.580
really painful to understand.

00:10:57.580 --> 00:11:00.008
It's got a lot of wonderful
information on it,

00:11:00.008 --> 00:11:02.300
but it's really hard to use,
really hard to understand,

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:04.510
really hard to get
any value out of it.

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:07.888
So and what's furthermore,
even if you found a way

00:11:07.888 --> 00:11:10.180
to parse it, OK, let's parse
this, this is fairly easy,

00:11:10.180 --> 00:11:12.280
JSON, just parse
it, you're still

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:15.220
left with these type of
elements, complex data

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:22.680
elements, often seen as
arrays or key value pairs.

00:11:22.680 --> 00:11:24.703
Now, this happens,
not just in the files

00:11:24.703 --> 00:11:26.120
but this also
happens in BigQuery.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:29.000
I'm sure many folks are familiar
with nested fields in BigQuery.

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:32.660
You'll see this type of
data nested inside of it.

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:34.940
So you'll still
encounter an issue

00:11:34.940 --> 00:11:38.330
where, hey, after I've done the
first level of understanding

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:41.010
the data, I need to
go deeper than that.

00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:44.000
And finally, even if you
get to individual values,

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.640
let's say, for example, I get
to the search reference here

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:48.890
and I get to my
individual value here,

00:11:48.890 --> 00:11:52.187
well, really, you don't want
to access the whole thing,

00:11:52.187 --> 00:11:54.770
you actually want to get access
to some of the embedded values

00:11:54.770 --> 00:11:55.312
inside of it.

00:11:59.180 --> 00:12:01.447
This clicker's slightly off.

00:12:01.447 --> 00:12:03.030
So what you want to
do is you actually

00:12:03.030 --> 00:12:04.830
want to extract specific parts.

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:06.900
In this case here, you're
looking for the search

00:12:06.900 --> 00:12:08.255
term that was actually used.

00:12:08.255 --> 00:12:09.380
So that's an embedded case.

00:12:09.380 --> 00:12:10.963
So once again, it's
still nested data,

00:12:10.963 --> 00:12:13.980
even though you're down
to the individual value.

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:17.960
So how does Dataprep
handle this scenario?

00:12:17.960 --> 00:12:19.960
Well, first thing out of
the box, Cloud Dataprep

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:21.882
supports those
various file formats

00:12:21.882 --> 00:12:24.340
and will immediately show you
that data in a row and column

00:12:24.340 --> 00:12:25.330
format.

00:12:25.330 --> 00:12:27.217
So you immediately see
these elements here.

00:12:27.217 --> 00:12:28.300
I'll show-- point at this.

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:30.320
The elements here--
the customer--

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:32.182
you'll see them as
individual columns.

00:12:32.182 --> 00:12:34.390
You'll also see the data
types automatically inferred

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:36.130
in the case where you
have nested values,

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:38.920
this key value pair here, you'll
see that automatically show up

00:12:38.920 --> 00:12:42.800
here, and then the rest of the
data broken out from there.

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:45.520
Now, the nice part about this
is that, because Dataprep

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:47.590
has understood this
as a nested value,

00:12:47.590 --> 00:12:50.830
it actually can help you inspect
the contents by simply clicking

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:54.940
on it and you'll
be able to see--

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:59.133
you'll be able to see
a nicely formatted--

00:12:59.133 --> 00:13:01.300
you'll be able to see the
individual elements nicely

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:02.560
formatted.

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:04.313
So this is important
because, hey, this

00:13:04.313 --> 00:13:05.730
is information I'm
looking to have

00:13:05.730 --> 00:13:08.470
and I want to actually use
this for my transformations.

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:10.220
So very easily you
will be able to decide,

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:11.500
do I want expand this as rows?

00:13:11.500 --> 00:13:12.910
Or I expand this as columns?

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:15.430
This case, I kind of want
to unnest this and expand

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:16.570
this as columns.

00:13:16.570 --> 00:13:19.360
So just one click and
then I can just unnest it.

00:13:19.360 --> 00:13:23.350
And immediately it becomes five
columns in my same data set.

00:13:23.350 --> 00:13:25.630
So now you can perform your
aggregation, your joins,

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:27.890
whatever you need.

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:29.650
Now, this is a scenario
where I actually

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:32.360
wanted to expand it out.

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:36.200
But funny enough, a key trick
is that not all your data

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:37.250
is relevant.

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:39.080
One of the hard
parts of nested data

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:41.200
is that then there's a
lot of data inside of it

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:44.560
and sometimes it's all garbage,
or a lot of it's garbage,

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:46.700
and expanding and
unpacking all of it

00:13:46.700 --> 00:13:48.140
may be a waste of your time.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:50.768
It may be slow, actually
may cause more confusion.

00:13:50.768 --> 00:13:52.310
So there are times
where you actually

00:13:52.310 --> 00:13:54.860
want to be much more
specific about the data

00:13:54.860 --> 00:13:56.750
that you extract out.

00:13:56.750 --> 00:13:59.430
So I showed you an example
where I did expand it out.

00:13:59.430 --> 00:14:02.030
But let me show you
one now where I have,

00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:05.150
once again, I have these
key value pairs here--

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:06.985
so this is a sale
[INAUDIBLE] line.

00:14:06.985 --> 00:14:09.110
I've got some interesting
information inside of it.

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:09.810
That's great.

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:11.270
But I don't need all this.

00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:15.170
I really just want product
key, and let's say, total sale.

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:18.530
So what you can actually do as
part of the bar chart up here,

00:14:18.530 --> 00:14:20.620
Dataprep allows you
to actually select

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:22.370
those individual
elements, the things that

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:23.412
are highlighted in green.

00:14:23.412 --> 00:14:26.500
Just select the
elements that you need,

00:14:26.500 --> 00:14:31.180
and you can actually tell
Dataprep just to extract those.

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:32.912
And it'll just produce
those two columns.

00:14:32.912 --> 00:14:34.870
So you don't need to
expand everything and just

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:35.890
kind of blow out your data set.

00:14:35.890 --> 00:14:37.307
Just get the things
that you need,

00:14:37.307 --> 00:14:39.630
because you can see
what's inside of it.

00:14:39.630 --> 00:14:42.123
Other things you can
do with nested elements

00:14:42.123 --> 00:14:44.290
is that you can actually
perform metadata operation.

00:14:44.290 --> 00:14:45.630
Sometimes you might
ask the question like,

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:47.790
how many elements are
in this nested value?

00:14:47.790 --> 00:14:49.773
And you want to use
that in your data.

00:14:49.773 --> 00:14:51.690
You can also perform
those operations directly

00:14:51.690 --> 00:14:54.390
on these objects.

00:14:54.390 --> 00:14:57.660
The last one I want to show
is just talking about those--

00:14:57.660 --> 00:15:00.070
getting that substring
of information.

00:15:00.070 --> 00:15:01.770
So in this example
here, I wanted

00:15:01.770 --> 00:15:03.203
to grab that search term--

00:15:03.203 --> 00:15:04.620
I wanted to grab
that search term.

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:06.703
So what you'll see here
is that all you have to do

00:15:06.703 --> 00:15:08.950
is highlight the data
that you're interested in,

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:11.160
so you interact directly
with it, highlight it,

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:13.800
and Dataprep will automatically
suggest a transformation

00:15:13.800 --> 00:15:15.220
that extracts it out for you.

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:17.220
So you don't have to deal
with the whole string,

00:15:17.220 --> 00:15:19.290
you're just dealing
with what you need.

00:15:19.290 --> 00:15:19.930
All right.

00:15:19.930 --> 00:15:24.090
So now we've got our
data expanded, extracted.

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:26.870
Let's take a look
at the data itself.

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:28.520
And when you're
dealing with data,

00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:30.853
when you're looking at the
individual parts of the data,

00:15:30.853 --> 00:15:33.350
you're probably looking at
patterns inside of it, right?

00:15:33.350 --> 00:15:35.300
And I'm sure everyone's
familiar with these.

00:15:35.300 --> 00:15:38.000
These are pretty
standard examples.

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:39.830
These are pretty
standard examples.

00:15:39.830 --> 00:15:41.930
But of course, it depends
on your geography,

00:15:41.930 --> 00:15:46.160
depends on where your data
is from, that these standards

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:47.840
apply, right?

00:15:47.840 --> 00:15:50.125
And problems arise when
you have data coming in

00:15:50.125 --> 00:15:51.500
from different
places that you're

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:53.720
combining and
joining and blending

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:55.430
and they have different formats.

00:15:55.430 --> 00:15:56.147
You can't use it.

00:15:56.147 --> 00:15:57.730
Like, if these things
are-- it's going

00:15:57.730 --> 00:15:59.272
to be confusing if
you try to combine

00:15:59.272 --> 00:16:01.970
these things in the same
data set because they're just

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:03.660
different values.

00:16:03.660 --> 00:16:07.280
So the problem, of course,
is that parsing this, even

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:10.940
identifying these patterns
inside your data is not easy.

00:16:10.940 --> 00:16:13.190
Everyone here familiar
with regular expressions?

00:16:13.190 --> 00:16:14.720
Who loves regular
expressions here?

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:16.420
Just by a quick show of hands?

00:16:16.420 --> 00:16:16.920
All right.

00:16:16.920 --> 00:16:18.590
So you don't need
to be here for this.

00:16:18.590 --> 00:16:19.090
Thanks, bye.

00:16:22.615 --> 00:16:24.740
For everyone else, this is
going to be a challenge.

00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:27.020
This is going to be a
challenge to not only identify

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:29.330
the patterns that you
want, but even then,

00:16:29.330 --> 00:16:32.610
can you imagine how do you
convert all the patterns

00:16:32.610 --> 00:16:33.860
into a standard that you want?

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:36.590
You're going to be dealing with
multiple regular expressions

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:38.750
on that.

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:41.660
So the nice part is
that Dataprep actually

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:42.770
handles this--

00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:45.860
actually has more built in
tool specifically around data

00:16:45.860 --> 00:16:46.940
patterns.

00:16:46.940 --> 00:16:50.720
What you see here is
Dataprep taking a column,

00:16:50.720 --> 00:16:53.990
and you can see additional
column details here

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:56.575
for a selected column.

00:16:56.575 --> 00:16:58.700
Not only does it tell you
it's data quality up top,

00:16:58.700 --> 00:17:00.492
it tells you unique
value and distribution,

00:17:00.492 --> 00:17:02.570
but down here it
actually breaks out

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:06.200
all the pattern combinations
that are within the data itself

00:17:06.200 --> 00:17:08.030
and shows you the cardinality.

00:17:08.030 --> 00:17:12.297
So now you know what's in it
and how often does it appear.

00:17:12.297 --> 00:17:13.880
This is super useful,
because then you

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:16.448
can figure out which format
do you want to standardize

00:17:16.448 --> 00:17:17.990
the data on, which
format do you want

00:17:17.990 --> 00:17:19.490
to normalize and put things on.

00:17:19.490 --> 00:17:22.069
In this case, although
the majority of the data

00:17:22.069 --> 00:17:24.170
is in year, year,
month, day, I actually

00:17:24.170 --> 00:17:27.050
want it to be in month, month,
day, day, year, year, year.

00:17:27.050 --> 00:17:28.740
So that's kind of what I want.

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:32.000
So it's as simple as
clicking on the pattern

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:36.110
that you're interested in
and you'll get a suggestion

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:37.280
for transforming that data.

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:40.580
That automatically takes
all the patterns here,

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:43.040
converts all these other
value patterns here

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:45.847
into the pattern
format that you want.

00:17:45.847 --> 00:17:47.930
And all the syntaxes handle
them under the covers.

00:17:47.930 --> 00:17:49.120
So no regular
expression for you.

00:17:49.120 --> 00:17:51.100
So sorry-- whoever-- the
few people who wanted

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:52.900
this, no regular
expressions for you.

00:17:56.070 --> 00:17:58.270
At this point, it's probably
enough of me talking.

00:17:58.270 --> 00:17:59.470
You probably want to
see this in action.

00:17:59.470 --> 00:18:01.845
So I'm going to hand it over
to Cindy, so she can show it

00:18:01.845 --> 00:18:02.780
to you in the product.

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:03.697
CINDY SOOD: All right.

00:18:03.697 --> 00:18:05.460
Thanks, Sean.

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:09.690
So here we are in the
Cloud Dataprep home page.

00:18:09.690 --> 00:18:12.210
And under my Recently
Updated Items,

00:18:12.210 --> 00:18:15.330
I'm just going to choose
the Sales Data Flow.

00:18:15.330 --> 00:18:18.960
When I do that, it takes
us into a flow view.

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:22.950
And think of the flow as
a project and just a way

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:24.780
to organize your work.

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:27.690
I've already registered a
data set here in this flow.

00:18:27.690 --> 00:18:31.060
I can select it to see more
details over here on the side.

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:37.350
So we'll see that this is a
JSON file and it resides in GCS,

00:18:37.350 --> 00:18:41.230
and there's additional
metadata here as well.

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:44.650
Now, I've already started
doing some transformations.

00:18:44.650 --> 00:18:48.000
So I have some transformation
logic in my recipe

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:49.963
that's attached to the data.

00:18:49.963 --> 00:18:51.630
We're going to choose
to edit the recipe

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:55.050
and we're going to add
more transformation logic.

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:56.790
When we do that, were
brought into what

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:59.670
we refer to as a
transformer grid,

00:18:59.670 --> 00:19:04.060
and you're presented with
rows and columns of your data.

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:06.030
Now, I want you to
keep in mind, when

00:19:06.030 --> 00:19:07.920
we looked at the details
of that data set,

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:10.140
it was a JSON file,
remember that?

00:19:10.140 --> 00:19:13.470
And do you notice how
Dataprep has automatically

00:19:13.470 --> 00:19:17.430
unnested the file format into
rows and columns for you?

00:19:17.430 --> 00:19:18.410
That's pretty cool.

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:24.290
We've also done some really
other great things too.

00:19:24.290 --> 00:19:27.270
So you're also presented with
the profile of your data.

00:19:27.270 --> 00:19:31.730
So we do that with these
bar charts and histograms.

00:19:31.730 --> 00:19:34.650
And that's for every single
column that you have.

00:19:34.650 --> 00:19:36.530
And we also give you
this quality bar,

00:19:36.530 --> 00:19:38.000
which is kind of nice as well.

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:41.930
And this is based on
the inferred data type,

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:44.090
that Dataprep will infer
a data type for you when

00:19:44.090 --> 00:19:44.820
you load data.

00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:48.170
So we're going to give
you a quality bar that

00:19:48.170 --> 00:19:50.690
will tell you how many
valid or mismatched values

00:19:50.690 --> 00:19:53.150
you have present.

00:19:53.150 --> 00:19:55.580
So what's the first
thing you notice?

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:58.830
There's a big red flag
in this data here.

00:19:58.830 --> 00:20:00.920
So the first thing I notice
are these red sections

00:20:00.920 --> 00:20:02.990
of the quality bar,
so it's saying,

00:20:02.990 --> 00:20:05.810
come, take a look at me,
there's something wrong here.

00:20:05.810 --> 00:20:08.840
So, for an example,
under Currency Key,

00:20:08.840 --> 00:20:11.640
my data type is
inferred as an integer.

00:20:11.640 --> 00:20:13.550
Now I can show that
I have valid values

00:20:13.550 --> 00:20:17.810
present by the green
section of that quality bar.

00:20:17.810 --> 00:20:20.903
And then I have mismatched
values present as well.

00:20:20.903 --> 00:20:22.820
So this tells me that I
have some data in here

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:25.260
that's actually not an integer.

00:20:25.260 --> 00:20:28.580
So it flags us and it's a
really nice visual cue to say

00:20:28.580 --> 00:20:30.110
there's issues with the data.

00:20:30.110 --> 00:20:33.120
To deal with that, I'm
just going to click on it.

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:35.990
So you can interact with these
histograms and the quality

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:37.310
bars.

00:20:37.310 --> 00:20:38.810
And when I do
that, I'm basically

00:20:38.810 --> 00:20:40.230
interacting with the data.

00:20:40.230 --> 00:20:42.680
So when you interact with the
data, what we're going to do

00:20:42.680 --> 00:20:45.870
is we're going to come up with
these predictive interactions.

00:20:45.870 --> 00:20:48.860
So Dataprep has a predictive
interaction engine.

00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:51.260
Based on your interaction and
the data you're looking at,

00:20:51.260 --> 00:20:53.810
you're going to get these
suggestions over here.

00:20:53.810 --> 00:20:56.392
The first suggestion that I
get was to delete these rows.

00:20:56.392 --> 00:20:58.100
I don't really want
to delete these rows.

00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:00.300
What I want to do is I want
to deal with this data.

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:03.920
So I'm going to come down
here to this set suggestion

00:21:03.920 --> 00:21:07.430
and I have this set
suggestion that says,

00:21:07.430 --> 00:21:10.820
set the mismatched
values to zero.

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:14.900
When I choose it, I get
a really nice preview

00:21:14.900 --> 00:21:18.235
before I actually add
that logic to my recipe.

00:21:18.235 --> 00:21:19.610
So it helps you
make decisions on

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:21.985
whether this is something you
actually want to do or not.

00:21:21.985 --> 00:21:23.750
So this is fantastic.

00:21:23.750 --> 00:21:28.490
So I actually do want this,
so I'm just going to hit Add.

00:21:28.490 --> 00:21:33.260
You see a nice cleaned up
quality bar right here.

00:21:33.260 --> 00:21:36.905
And that logic is
added to my recipe.

00:21:36.905 --> 00:21:38.530
Now, you can also
interact with the bar

00:21:38.530 --> 00:21:41.650
charts of the histograms, as
well as interacting directly

00:21:41.650 --> 00:21:42.290
with the data.

00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:45.790
So here I'm going to select
this comma in my customer name

00:21:45.790 --> 00:21:48.830
because I want to split out the
last name from the first name.

00:21:48.830 --> 00:21:51.030
So all I have to do is
interact with that comma.

00:21:51.030 --> 00:21:54.350
Again, I get
suggestions over here.

00:21:54.350 --> 00:21:58.180
I get a split suggestion
as the first one.

00:21:58.180 --> 00:22:00.970
The first suggestion is just
to say, OK, any delimiter,

00:22:00.970 --> 00:22:04.640
but I'm going to specify
the comma as the delimiter.

00:22:04.640 --> 00:22:06.550
Again, we get a nice preview.

00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:07.825
This is exactly what I want.

00:22:07.825 --> 00:22:08.950
I'm just going to add that.

00:22:11.850 --> 00:22:14.270
Now we're to come over here
to the sales order line.

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:16.150
Now, this is a bit trickier.

00:22:16.150 --> 00:22:19.570
So here the data
type is an array.

00:22:19.570 --> 00:22:22.800
Now, an array in Dataprep,
as many of you probably know,

00:22:22.800 --> 00:22:27.240
is just a list of items
or a group of elements.

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:29.310
I'm going to expand
to look at this.

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:35.260
So not the prettiest thing.

00:22:35.260 --> 00:22:37.690
To help visualize, I'm
going to search my rows

00:22:37.690 --> 00:22:41.200
just for an open curly bracket.

00:22:41.200 --> 00:22:45.940
So when I do that, I can see
the start of each element,

00:22:45.940 --> 00:22:47.930
or each item in this array.

00:22:47.930 --> 00:22:50.860
So this is really helpful
because I can visualize it.

00:22:50.860 --> 00:22:53.110
But what I really
need to do here

00:22:53.110 --> 00:22:56.020
is inspect the information
that's associated

00:22:56.020 --> 00:22:58.260
with the sales order lines.

00:22:58.260 --> 00:23:01.870
And I have to get each of
these individual elements

00:23:01.870 --> 00:23:04.135
into their own unique row.

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:10.650
So that's really easy
to do in Dataprep.

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:14.310
All I'm going to do is come over
here to the sales order line,

00:23:14.310 --> 00:23:16.200
select it.

00:23:16.200 --> 00:23:17.850
Again, we do get some
details over here

00:23:17.850 --> 00:23:22.140
about the data, as
well as suggestions.

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:24.420
I'm going to come down to
this flatten array values

00:23:24.420 --> 00:23:25.860
into new rows.

00:23:25.860 --> 00:23:27.680
I'm going to select that.

00:23:27.680 --> 00:23:31.590
It says convert arrays in
the sales order line to rows.

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:34.480
And what that does, and
you can see in the preview,

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:37.230
is it's going to
take each element

00:23:37.230 --> 00:23:39.360
in that array, those
lists of items,

00:23:39.360 --> 00:23:40.980
and put it into its own rows.

00:23:40.980 --> 00:23:43.320
And I did it with just
the click of a button.

00:23:43.320 --> 00:23:46.560
And you can see all
of these elements

00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:48.930
from that list put
into their own row,

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:52.110
as well as all of the other
relevant data associated

00:23:52.110 --> 00:23:54.270
with that row is populated.

00:23:56.810 --> 00:23:58.240
So I'm just going to add this.

00:23:58.240 --> 00:24:00.070
It's exactly what I want.

00:24:00.070 --> 00:24:02.740
I'm going to add
that to my recipe.

00:24:02.740 --> 00:24:06.650
You come back to that column,
we have a new data type.

00:24:06.650 --> 00:24:09.290
We have an object.

00:24:09.290 --> 00:24:12.350
The object is basically
these key value pair.

00:24:12.350 --> 00:24:15.550
So you can see that because
you can expand the data

00:24:15.550 --> 00:24:17.960
and you can see all
of these keys here

00:24:17.960 --> 00:24:21.020
and all the values
associated with the keys.

00:24:21.020 --> 00:24:22.510
Now, as Sean had
talked about, we

00:24:22.510 --> 00:24:24.790
don't want to extract
all of that information.

00:24:24.790 --> 00:24:28.780
I'm only interested in a couple
of items within the sales order

00:24:28.780 --> 00:24:29.780
line.

00:24:29.780 --> 00:24:31.240
So what I'm going
to do now is I'm

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:35.470
going to extract just a couple
of these key value pairs.

00:24:35.470 --> 00:24:39.110
And to do that, I'm going
to come to the histogram.

00:24:39.110 --> 00:24:43.510
I'm going to select this
bar chart that represents

00:24:43.510 --> 00:24:45.670
the product key,
and then the bar

00:24:45.670 --> 00:24:49.960
chart that represents
the unit price.

00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:51.640
We get a nice preview.

00:24:51.640 --> 00:24:55.570
The suggestion, of course,
is to unnest and create

00:24:55.570 --> 00:24:58.180
new columns for these keys.

00:24:58.180 --> 00:24:59.987
So the product key
becomes the header,

00:24:59.987 --> 00:25:01.570
and the unit price
becomes the header.

00:25:01.570 --> 00:25:04.780
And then all of the values
associated with those keys

00:25:04.780 --> 00:25:08.760
are now populated in your rows.

00:25:08.760 --> 00:25:10.270
It's exactly what I want.

00:25:10.270 --> 00:25:12.580
I'm going to add it.

00:25:12.580 --> 00:25:17.350
And now, basically, I have
expanded my data, the rows

00:25:17.350 --> 00:25:20.740
and the columns, by just
flattening and then extracting

00:25:20.740 --> 00:25:22.010
the relevant information.

00:25:22.010 --> 00:25:24.880
So now I'm done pulling
out the information

00:25:24.880 --> 00:25:26.235
that I'm most interested in.

00:25:26.235 --> 00:25:27.610
SEAN MA: That
looked really easy,

00:25:27.610 --> 00:25:29.830
just half a dozen steps or so.

00:25:29.830 --> 00:25:32.300
CINDY SOOD: Well, Sean, if
you were paying attention,

00:25:32.300 --> 00:25:34.686
you would see that it
was only two steps.

00:25:34.686 --> 00:25:35.360
SEAN MA: My bad.

00:25:38.303 --> 00:25:39.220
CINDY SOOD: All right.

00:25:39.220 --> 00:25:42.790
I'm going to continue
with this data,

00:25:42.790 --> 00:25:44.980
because over here
in the order date

00:25:44.980 --> 00:25:47.440
I see that I still have
some mismatched values,

00:25:47.440 --> 00:25:51.730
and I need to finish
prepping my data.

00:25:51.730 --> 00:25:54.820
So, again, I could just
select this red section

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:57.100
to see what's mismatched.

00:25:57.100 --> 00:25:59.890
You then are-- you're
given all of the rows.

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:01.840
When you select that,
it's going to highlight

00:26:01.840 --> 00:26:03.820
all of the rows that
are mismatched value.

00:26:03.820 --> 00:26:06.760
And I can kind of visually
see right here that I just

00:26:06.760 --> 00:26:08.167
have different date formats.

00:26:08.167 --> 00:26:10.000
So what I really want
to do with this column

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:13.820
is to get everything into
the same date format.

00:26:13.820 --> 00:26:17.190
Again, it's pretty easy
to do this in Dataprep.

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:19.680
I'm going to select that column.

00:26:19.680 --> 00:26:22.280
Over here, those details again.

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:25.320
And then we have this section
that's called Patterns.

00:26:25.320 --> 00:26:27.420
Now, I can choose to
show pattern details.

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:29.680
Will give you these
patterns right here.

00:26:29.680 --> 00:26:32.750
It will tell the number of rows.

00:26:32.750 --> 00:26:37.240
And then you get a little
sample of data as well.

00:26:37.240 --> 00:26:41.380
So I actually want to go month,
day, day, year, year, year,

00:26:41.380 --> 00:26:42.430
year.

00:26:42.430 --> 00:26:44.380
I'm going to choose
that pattern.

00:26:44.380 --> 00:26:46.810
Now, again, I just
interacted with the data.

00:26:46.810 --> 00:26:49.660
I get these suggestions over
here on the right panel.

00:26:49.660 --> 00:26:51.280
The first suggestion
is keep rows.

00:26:51.280 --> 00:26:52.480
That's actually
what we're seeing,

00:26:52.480 --> 00:26:54.480
but I'm going to come
down to this convert rows.

00:26:57.800 --> 00:27:00.350
And if you look
here, it's converting

00:27:00.350 --> 00:27:04.370
values like these three that
I don't want to that one

00:27:04.370 --> 00:27:09.030
that I actually selected
in the patterns.

00:27:09.030 --> 00:27:10.740
So three to one in one step.

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:12.370
So I'm going to hit Add.

00:27:12.370 --> 00:27:15.800
And when I do that,
this gets updated

00:27:15.800 --> 00:27:19.860
with the standardized
format that I selected.

00:27:19.860 --> 00:27:22.740
Pretty nice.

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:27.570
So I was able to
profile, identify

00:27:27.570 --> 00:27:31.890
the pattern of interest,
change it, and then validate,

00:27:31.890 --> 00:27:34.980
because if you look now, we
have that quality bar here

00:27:34.980 --> 00:27:36.495
and it's all valid values.

00:27:40.250 --> 00:27:42.580
So that's the end of the
first part of the demo.

00:27:42.580 --> 00:27:44.290
We're going to go
back to Sean and he's

00:27:44.290 --> 00:27:45.950
going to talk about
a couple more.

00:27:45.950 --> 00:27:47.230
SEAN MA: I think there
were two people here

00:27:47.230 --> 00:27:49.600
who were sad you didn't do
regular expressions, so--

00:27:49.600 --> 00:27:50.410
CINDY SOOD: Oh, no.

00:27:50.410 --> 00:27:53.330
You want me to have a headache?

00:27:53.330 --> 00:27:54.070
SEAN MA: OK.

00:27:54.070 --> 00:27:57.070
So we're moving on to
the next two examples

00:27:57.070 --> 00:28:00.430
with inconsistent data
and results validation.

00:28:00.430 --> 00:28:01.570
We just covered patterns.

00:28:01.570 --> 00:28:03.873
So what the hell's inconsistent
data supposed to mean?

00:28:03.873 --> 00:28:05.290
Well, that's
actually specifically

00:28:05.290 --> 00:28:09.550
data that doesn't really
conform to any patterns.

00:28:09.550 --> 00:28:12.060
So quick example here of--

00:28:12.060 --> 00:28:15.120
here's a company that, of
course, humans have entered--

00:28:15.120 --> 00:28:18.090
ACME Inc. You have slightly
different variations

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:18.900
of the data.

00:28:18.900 --> 00:28:21.540
You see one with
periods and a hyphen.

00:28:21.540 --> 00:28:24.750
I think spell checker kicked in
and just renamed this to acne.

00:28:24.750 --> 00:28:26.970
And then we've got
nice formatting,

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:29.670
got bolds and quotes that
you have inside of this.

00:28:29.670 --> 00:28:32.130
So while we can all
recognize this is probably

00:28:32.130 --> 00:28:34.177
all the same company, not
so great for your data,

00:28:34.177 --> 00:28:36.510
and not so great if you're
going to do analysis on this.

00:28:36.510 --> 00:28:39.250
So it's really hard
to pick this up.

00:28:39.250 --> 00:28:41.610
Of course, the goal has
then standardized this

00:28:41.610 --> 00:28:43.170
to a single
established value, so

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:45.930
that you can make effective
use of joins and aggregations,

00:28:45.930 --> 00:28:50.250
because you do want ACME Inc to
show up four times as one time

00:28:50.250 --> 00:28:54.140
as opposed to four
different companies aside.

00:28:54.140 --> 00:28:56.570
So this is something
new that we've

00:28:56.570 --> 00:29:01.250
added to Dataprep
recently, is the ability

00:29:01.250 --> 00:29:05.410
to cluster your data into groups
based on clustering algorithms.

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:08.150
This is something we released
just a few weeks ago.

00:29:08.150 --> 00:29:10.760
And in this example here, what
you'll see is that we have

00:29:10.760 --> 00:29:14.780
several different clustering
algorithms that will grab

00:29:14.780 --> 00:29:17.420
the data-- that will
access the data--

00:29:17.420 --> 00:29:19.730
that will take the
data in a given column

00:29:19.730 --> 00:29:22.680
and show to you how
many rows show up

00:29:22.680 --> 00:29:26.990
in a-- for a given instance
of that data, kind of group

00:29:26.990 --> 00:29:28.107
them all together for you.

00:29:28.107 --> 00:29:29.690
There are different
ways you can group

00:29:29.690 --> 00:29:33.240
the data, based on comparing
values, closer spelling,

00:29:33.240 --> 00:29:35.003
sometimes pronunciation.

00:29:35.003 --> 00:29:37.420
And that's really important,
because the trick to all this

00:29:37.420 --> 00:29:39.730
is that you don't want to apply
just one algorithm when you're

00:29:39.730 --> 00:29:41.230
defining your
groups, you actually

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:43.075
want to apply multiple
algorithms on this.

00:29:43.075 --> 00:29:44.950
The reason why is you
want to capture as many

00:29:44.950 --> 00:29:46.750
of these inconsistency
as possible,

00:29:46.750 --> 00:29:48.070
so you can standardize them.

00:29:48.070 --> 00:29:51.940
So for example, apply
additional algorithms

00:29:51.940 --> 00:29:54.610
on this, multiple of them,
and you'll see more come out.

00:29:54.610 --> 00:29:56.450
This one I applied--
was pronunciation,

00:29:56.450 --> 00:29:59.240
so you'll see things
like acne and acme.

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:01.360
These pronounce
similarly, so you actually

00:30:01.360 --> 00:30:03.760
want to include them
into your group.

00:30:03.760 --> 00:30:05.225
It just makes
things a lot easier

00:30:05.225 --> 00:30:07.600
for you to then standardize
it and go forward from there.

00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:10.403
You can type in what you
want your end result to be.

00:30:10.403 --> 00:30:12.070
Now, all this said,
wonderful algorithms

00:30:12.070 --> 00:30:14.300
that we are constantly
making better and better.

00:30:14.300 --> 00:30:15.895
It's never ever
going to be perfect.

00:30:15.895 --> 00:30:18.520
So one of the nice part of being
able to interact with the data

00:30:18.520 --> 00:30:21.280
and actually see this
is that you can now

00:30:21.280 --> 00:30:24.500
also identify when there
are exceptions to the rule.

00:30:24.500 --> 00:30:26.440
So now, as an example,
where the clustering,

00:30:26.440 --> 00:30:29.710
because of the pronunciation,
it captured one too many.

00:30:29.710 --> 00:30:32.770
In this case, I was just looking
for BB Construction, but not

00:30:32.770 --> 00:30:33.850
Bibby Construction.

00:30:33.850 --> 00:30:34.990
That's not what I wanted.

00:30:34.990 --> 00:30:37.600
So you actually will be
able to go into Dataprep,

00:30:37.600 --> 00:30:39.997
select that exception,
just highlight it and say,

00:30:39.997 --> 00:30:41.830
I don't want to include
this, so I can still

00:30:41.830 --> 00:30:43.122
standardize on everything else.

00:30:43.122 --> 00:30:45.010
So it also captures
exceptions in addition

00:30:45.010 --> 00:30:48.400
to the standard set.

00:30:48.400 --> 00:30:50.530
So now we're at a
point where we've

00:30:50.530 --> 00:30:52.600
gone through most
of the challenges,

00:30:52.600 --> 00:30:56.320
we've addressed the
structured and formatted data,

00:30:56.320 --> 00:30:59.590
we've made sure that
we've cleansed the data,

00:30:59.590 --> 00:31:02.770
you've probably performed
a bunch of transformations,

00:31:02.770 --> 00:31:07.780
a bunch of transformations to
pivot the data, apply formulas.

00:31:07.780 --> 00:31:10.490
The last thing we want to cover
again was the data validation.

00:31:10.490 --> 00:31:12.157
How do you know that
what you just built

00:31:12.157 --> 00:31:15.770
is actually going to
be useful downstream?

00:31:15.770 --> 00:31:18.130
So when it comes to
the target, you'll

00:31:18.130 --> 00:31:21.340
often see examples like
this where the targets have

00:31:21.340 --> 00:31:22.600
a very well designed schema.

00:31:22.600 --> 00:31:24.630
This is an example
of a BigQuery table.

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:26.020
Everyone's familiar with this.

00:31:26.020 --> 00:31:28.500
Columns, data
types, constraints.

00:31:28.500 --> 00:31:30.610
Pretty standard in
that regard, right?

00:31:30.610 --> 00:31:33.145
And most of the time, when
you're preparing data,

00:31:33.145 --> 00:31:35.020
you probably have a good
sense of what you're

00:31:35.020 --> 00:31:37.600
looking for, you're going to--

00:31:37.600 --> 00:31:40.200
manually compare, or you're
going to remember it off

00:31:40.200 --> 00:31:41.847
of memory, what you want.

00:31:41.847 --> 00:31:43.930
And you decide, OK, I think
this is what I wanted.

00:31:43.930 --> 00:31:46.480
I'm going to go ahead and
publish this to, again,

00:31:46.480 --> 00:31:48.910
I'm going to use
BigQuery in this example.

00:31:48.910 --> 00:31:51.970
And oops, BigQuery
greets you with a very

00:31:51.970 --> 00:31:54.820
specific and accurate
error message.

00:31:54.820 --> 00:31:58.953
And while it's got all
the information you want,

00:31:58.953 --> 00:32:01.120
it's actually very difficult
to parse and figure out

00:32:01.120 --> 00:32:01.930
what went wrong.

00:32:01.930 --> 00:32:03.353
You might be going
back and forth

00:32:03.353 --> 00:32:05.020
trying to figure out,
well, what's wrong

00:32:05.020 --> 00:32:07.020
my Dataprep, what's wrong
in the BigQuery table,

00:32:07.020 --> 00:32:09.750
and you kind of want to go
back and forth between the two

00:32:09.750 --> 00:32:10.960
and figure that thing out.

00:32:10.960 --> 00:32:14.190
I will note that, while you see
this error message in BigQuery,

00:32:14.190 --> 00:32:16.130
you see the same problem
with files as well.

00:32:16.130 --> 00:32:17.800
It's actually kind of worse,
because they don't tell you

00:32:17.800 --> 00:32:19.150
what the scheme
is supposed to be,

00:32:19.150 --> 00:32:21.233
so you won't find out until
you kind of pass along

00:32:21.233 --> 00:32:22.960
and you get bad data.

00:32:22.960 --> 00:32:25.450
Well, the good thing
is Dataprep allows

00:32:25.450 --> 00:32:29.720
you to do this comparison
much, much more easily.

00:32:29.720 --> 00:32:34.230
And you achieve that
by comparing the data--

00:32:34.230 --> 00:32:37.207
comparing the target directly
in the data set itself.

00:32:37.207 --> 00:32:38.790
So what you can do
is you can actually

00:32:38.790 --> 00:32:40.890
import the target here--

00:32:40.890 --> 00:32:43.330
import the target here
directly into a data set,

00:32:43.330 --> 00:32:45.330
and it will automatically
align against the data

00:32:45.330 --> 00:32:46.770
that you're working with.

00:32:46.770 --> 00:32:48.270
So you can do that
direct comparison

00:32:48.270 --> 00:32:51.180
and see samples of the data,
understand what that is.

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:54.030
This comparison is really
useful because then you can also

00:32:54.030 --> 00:32:56.193
get these indicators
that tell you, hey, how

00:32:56.193 --> 00:32:57.360
well have things matched up?

00:32:57.360 --> 00:32:59.220
So green indicating things
are very well matched up,

00:32:59.220 --> 00:33:00.660
that's exactly
what you want, blue

00:33:00.660 --> 00:33:02.383
indicating that there's
slight mismatches

00:33:02.383 --> 00:33:04.050
that you want to
address, red indicating

00:33:04.050 --> 00:33:05.220
there's something very--

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:07.480
something significantly wrong.

00:33:07.480 --> 00:33:10.170
And the last part about this
is, because now Dataprep

00:33:10.170 --> 00:33:12.540
knows what you want
it to look like,

00:33:12.540 --> 00:33:14.370
it actually can
suggest transformations

00:33:14.370 --> 00:33:16.432
to help fix this.

00:33:16.432 --> 00:33:18.640
So in this case, I'm going
to fix the effective date.

00:33:18.640 --> 00:33:19.740
I'm going to click
on that, and it's

00:33:19.740 --> 00:33:21.115
going to tell me,
oh, the problem

00:33:21.115 --> 00:33:24.000
with your effective date is
that your formats are incorrect,

00:33:24.000 --> 00:33:24.500
instead of--

00:33:24.500 --> 00:33:27.083
you want to change it from day,
day, month, month, year, year,

00:33:27.083 --> 00:33:28.200
back to year, year, month.

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:28.980
Great.

00:33:28.980 --> 00:33:29.640
That's what I want to do.

00:33:29.640 --> 00:33:31.180
So it's as simple
as clicking on it,

00:33:31.180 --> 00:33:33.780
and it will
automatically fix it.

00:33:33.780 --> 00:33:36.990
Now, there may be cases where
we're dealing with Y data sets,

00:33:36.990 --> 00:33:39.450
where you're dealing with
potentially hundreds of--

00:33:39.450 --> 00:33:43.350
sorry-- Y data sets, potentially
hundreds of columns across.

00:33:43.350 --> 00:33:45.060
In that situation,
what you can do

00:33:45.060 --> 00:33:48.150
is switch to the
column view of Dataprep

00:33:48.150 --> 00:33:52.470
and it allows you to more
compactly see just the columns.

00:33:52.470 --> 00:33:55.980
It's much easier to reorder,
rename columns on mass.

00:33:55.980 --> 00:34:00.120
You can also decide to remove
columns or add missing ones.

00:34:00.120 --> 00:34:01.800
And the whole goal,
of course, is then

00:34:01.800 --> 00:34:03.675
to make sure everything
matches, everything's

00:34:03.675 --> 00:34:06.473
good to go in green, and
validate this in the product

00:34:06.473 --> 00:34:07.890
before you go ahead
and publish it

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:10.610
before you set your automation.

00:34:10.610 --> 00:34:11.900
All right.

00:34:11.900 --> 00:34:14.400
So let's now see this in action.

00:34:14.400 --> 00:34:15.340
Cindy.

00:34:15.340 --> 00:34:16.228
CINDY SOOD: Great.

00:34:16.228 --> 00:34:17.020
Thanks again, Sean.

00:34:19.870 --> 00:34:22.370
Waiting for our
screen to come up.

00:34:22.370 --> 00:34:23.760
There we go.

00:34:23.760 --> 00:34:26.570
So I am back in Dataprep.

00:34:26.570 --> 00:34:28.730
And I'm in the transformer grid.

00:34:28.730 --> 00:34:31.040
And I have a different
data set here.

00:34:31.040 --> 00:34:32.780
And this is actually--

00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:34.010
it resides in GCS.

00:34:34.010 --> 00:34:36.980
And it's recently
uploaded customer data.

00:34:36.980 --> 00:34:39.330
So it has some information,
like their first name,

00:34:39.330 --> 00:34:42.469
their last name, their
account number, some employer

00:34:42.469 --> 00:34:45.239
information and many
other fields here.

00:34:45.239 --> 00:34:48.260
Now, what I need to do with this
is I need to take this data set

00:34:48.260 --> 00:34:52.940
and I need to append a customer
details table in BigQuery.

00:34:52.940 --> 00:34:55.850
So before I do that though,
I know I have some manually

00:34:55.850 --> 00:35:00.230
entered data here, so I have to
do some column standardization.

00:35:00.230 --> 00:35:02.870
And I need to do that
on this employer column

00:35:02.870 --> 00:35:05.810
because I have lots of
different company names here.

00:35:05.810 --> 00:35:08.752
And I'm sure you can imagine
some of the challenges

00:35:08.752 --> 00:35:10.460
with this manually
entered data if you're

00:35:10.460 --> 00:35:12.470
going to do some aggregations
and you don't have

00:35:12.470 --> 00:35:15.900
a single standardized value.

00:35:15.900 --> 00:35:17.810
So I'm going to come
to the employer column.

00:35:17.810 --> 00:35:20.300
We're going to go to
this drop down menu,

00:35:20.300 --> 00:35:23.060
and we have
standardized right here.

00:35:23.060 --> 00:35:24.920
So when I choose standardize--

00:35:24.920 --> 00:35:28.130
I'm just going to show you
where that clustering option is.

00:35:28.130 --> 00:35:31.460
We run the first cluster to
look at the similar strings

00:35:31.460 --> 00:35:32.660
across all the rows.

00:35:32.660 --> 00:35:35.060
So we're clustering
all of the rows

00:35:35.060 --> 00:35:38.690
based on characters in common.

00:35:38.690 --> 00:35:43.340
So here with Acme Inc I see
I have 10 different values.

00:35:43.340 --> 00:35:47.180
So Acme Inc written 10 different
times across 53 different rows.

00:35:47.180 --> 00:35:49.710
And that's really quite
a problem sometimes.

00:35:49.710 --> 00:35:51.950
So I mean, there
are things here--

00:35:51.950 --> 00:35:54.690
it's not just like
capitalization,

00:35:54.690 --> 00:35:57.530
but it's also hyphens
present, commas present,

00:35:57.530 --> 00:35:59.030
periods present--

00:35:59.030 --> 00:36:01.760
people who embellish their
company name when they're

00:36:01.760 --> 00:36:04.850
inputting it themselves--

00:36:04.850 --> 00:36:06.270
the quotes here.

00:36:06.270 --> 00:36:08.870
So I want to get all of
these to the same value.

00:36:08.870 --> 00:36:12.470
To do that, I'm going
to select them all.

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:13.460
We'll come over here.

00:36:13.460 --> 00:36:17.550
I'm going to enter my new value.

00:36:17.550 --> 00:36:19.480
Make it kind of simple.

00:36:19.480 --> 00:36:21.572
Now, before I apply
this, Sean talked

00:36:21.572 --> 00:36:23.280
about running another
algorithm, so we're

00:36:23.280 --> 00:36:25.200
going to come back to
the clustering options,

00:36:25.200 --> 00:36:28.960
and we're going to now cluster
values based on pronunciation,

00:36:28.960 --> 00:36:32.370
so all of the rows that
actually sound alike.

00:36:32.370 --> 00:36:33.870
And when I do that,
what do you see?

00:36:33.870 --> 00:36:37.230
We actually have a
few more rows show up.

00:36:37.230 --> 00:36:39.870
Now, the nice thing here
is all I have to do is come

00:36:39.870 --> 00:36:41.680
and click on these.

00:36:41.680 --> 00:36:45.810
And as I select them, the
value, that standardized value

00:36:45.810 --> 00:36:47.880
that I want is
automatically put in.

00:36:53.100 --> 00:36:54.140
It's that simple.

00:36:54.140 --> 00:36:57.560
I'm now going to hit Apply.

00:36:57.560 --> 00:37:01.085
We're now going to choose to add
this logic to my recipe step.

00:37:01.085 --> 00:37:02.960
So you can always come
back and look at this.

00:37:06.700 --> 00:37:09.110
And when we come back
to the transformer grid,

00:37:09.110 --> 00:37:10.720
the recipe opens up.

00:37:10.720 --> 00:37:13.240
And we now have
this reusable logic.

00:37:13.240 --> 00:37:15.185
So, anytime we have
new data coming in,

00:37:15.185 --> 00:37:17.560
we can actually use this logic
that we've already created

00:37:17.560 --> 00:37:19.580
to do exactly the same thing.

00:37:19.580 --> 00:37:20.590
So that's pretty nice.

00:37:23.750 --> 00:37:26.270
Now, if you remember what we
want to do with this data set,

00:37:26.270 --> 00:37:28.070
so we've done our
standardization,

00:37:28.070 --> 00:37:30.010
is I want to now
run a job, and I'm

00:37:30.010 --> 00:37:33.910
going to append that BigQuery
table, the Customer Details.

00:37:33.910 --> 00:37:37.510
So I'm going to come here,
I'm going to choose Run Job.

00:37:37.510 --> 00:37:39.700
And I already have my
publishing action set up,

00:37:39.700 --> 00:37:43.030
so append BigQuery,
the Customer Details.

00:37:43.030 --> 00:37:46.840
I'm going to run this
in US-West is fine.

00:37:46.840 --> 00:37:47.940
And I'll hit Run Job.

00:37:56.080 --> 00:37:56.650
OK.

00:37:56.650 --> 00:37:58.180
Look at what we have here.

00:37:58.180 --> 00:38:01.550
Something's not happy with
this, so we can't run the job.

00:38:01.550 --> 00:38:03.550
So what I've done is--

00:38:03.550 --> 00:38:05.330
I only paid attention
to the data.

00:38:05.330 --> 00:38:06.880
What did I not pay attention to?

00:38:06.880 --> 00:38:09.520
I didn't pay attention to
the schema in BigQuery,

00:38:09.520 --> 00:38:11.300
so I need to fix that.

00:38:11.300 --> 00:38:14.160
So I'm going to cancel.

00:38:14.160 --> 00:38:16.160
We'll close out of this recipe.

00:38:16.160 --> 00:38:18.280
Now, I have the schema
over here in BigQuery,

00:38:18.280 --> 00:38:20.858
so what I could do is
I can come down here,

00:38:20.858 --> 00:38:21.900
I can look at the schema.

00:38:25.604 --> 00:38:26.775
Just scroll down.

00:38:31.130 --> 00:38:32.090
Or not.

00:38:32.090 --> 00:38:35.760
They've changed my
resolution on the screen.

00:38:35.760 --> 00:38:37.310
So there's a schema down here.

00:38:37.310 --> 00:38:39.060
Here we see account number.

00:38:39.060 --> 00:38:40.310
It's an integer.

00:38:40.310 --> 00:38:43.070
So we'll come back to Dataprep.

00:38:43.070 --> 00:38:45.600
And, OK, here's
the account number.

00:38:45.600 --> 00:38:46.450
Here's the integer.

00:38:46.450 --> 00:38:49.328
Or it's a string though,
so I'd have to change that.

00:38:49.328 --> 00:38:51.620
So I could go back to BigQuery
and I could try and look

00:38:51.620 --> 00:38:52.980
at that schema again.

00:38:52.980 --> 00:38:55.110
Here we go.

00:38:55.110 --> 00:38:56.870
And then back to Dataprep.

00:38:56.870 --> 00:38:59.210
Well, that's kind of clunky
and not really a lot of fun.

00:38:59.210 --> 00:39:00.960
I don't really want
to do that, especially

00:39:00.960 --> 00:39:02.090
for a really wide data set.

00:39:02.090 --> 00:39:06.290
So what I'm going to do is I'm
going to load that target right

00:39:06.290 --> 00:39:09.060
here in my transformer grid.

00:39:09.060 --> 00:39:11.190
So I'm going to attach a target.

00:39:11.190 --> 00:39:14.150
I'm going to look for my
customer details, target

00:39:14.150 --> 00:39:18.600
schema, and I'm going to add it.

00:39:18.600 --> 00:39:19.640
So that's pretty nice.

00:39:19.640 --> 00:39:21.330
This is right from BigQuery.

00:39:21.330 --> 00:39:25.460
This is the table
that I want to update.

00:39:25.460 --> 00:39:28.010
And we're going to give you
a sample of the data as well.

00:39:28.010 --> 00:39:34.390
And we give you these visual
cues to help you get your--

00:39:34.390 --> 00:39:40.107
this source data to the
target schema that we want.

00:39:40.107 --> 00:39:41.690
Now, we also have a
column view, which

00:39:41.690 --> 00:39:44.410
is super helpful when
you're looking at wide data,

00:39:44.410 --> 00:39:46.230
it's a bit more efficient.

00:39:46.230 --> 00:39:46.910
So I go to the--

00:39:46.910 --> 00:39:48.830
I just clicked on
this column view here.

00:39:48.830 --> 00:39:49.940
Over here is our target.

00:39:49.940 --> 00:39:51.560
Over here is the source.

00:39:51.560 --> 00:39:53.150
Again, we have
these visual cues.

00:39:53.150 --> 00:39:55.340
The first position
is the account number

00:39:55.340 --> 00:39:56.690
and it's an integer.

00:39:56.690 --> 00:39:59.340
When I hover over
this blue box here,

00:39:59.340 --> 00:40:01.490
it's telling you there's
a matching column.

00:40:01.490 --> 00:40:04.550
I can move it, and it will
show you in the source

00:40:04.550 --> 00:40:06.470
where that column is.

00:40:06.470 --> 00:40:10.260
So watch real close.

00:40:10.260 --> 00:40:13.070
I just selected that blue
box and it automatically

00:40:13.070 --> 00:40:15.440
moved that account number.

00:40:15.440 --> 00:40:17.430
Does anybody notice
what else it did?

00:40:17.430 --> 00:40:19.070
There's a big hint.

00:40:19.070 --> 00:40:21.620
It automatically changed
it to an integer for me,

00:40:21.620 --> 00:40:23.870
so I don't have to go
in and do any of that.

00:40:23.870 --> 00:40:27.210
So now I'm just going
to continue down.

00:40:27.210 --> 00:40:31.770
City-- we have all of these
matches, which is fantastic.

00:40:31.770 --> 00:40:33.470
This is so easy.

00:40:33.470 --> 00:40:37.520
And then we come down here and
we have this contract start,

00:40:37.520 --> 00:40:39.430
and it's a red box.

00:40:39.430 --> 00:40:42.170
Now, if it doesn't automatically
detect this for me,

00:40:42.170 --> 00:40:44.300
I can choose what column.

00:40:44.300 --> 00:40:45.920
And I know I have
this column one--

00:40:45.920 --> 00:40:48.905
I actually know-- I can see
the data type is the same.

00:40:48.905 --> 00:40:50.530
And I happen to know
it is the contract

00:40:50.530 --> 00:40:53.120
start, so all I'm going
to do is scroll down

00:40:53.120 --> 00:40:55.460
and select that column one.

00:40:55.460 --> 00:40:58.250
It automatically
renames it for me,

00:40:58.250 --> 00:41:00.230
and we have a nice alignment.

00:41:00.230 --> 00:41:01.830
Now I have two extra columns.

00:41:01.830 --> 00:41:04.700
So this is why I got that big
error in addition to that one

00:41:04.700 --> 00:41:06.290
data type difference.

00:41:06.290 --> 00:41:09.080
I'm just going to select these.

00:41:09.080 --> 00:41:11.180
Again, I'm interacting
with the data.

00:41:11.180 --> 00:41:14.048
I get these suggestions
over here on the side.

00:41:14.048 --> 00:41:16.340
I'm going to come down to
the delete columns, because I

00:41:16.340 --> 00:41:17.180
don't want it.

00:41:17.180 --> 00:41:19.310
I'm going and choose Add.

00:41:19.310 --> 00:41:20.930
We'll go back to that grid view.

00:41:20.930 --> 00:41:23.980
Close out of this recipe.

00:41:23.980 --> 00:41:28.340
And nicely, I can validate
against my BigQuery table

00:41:28.340 --> 00:41:30.530
before I actually publish.

00:41:30.530 --> 00:41:31.392
Pretty awesome.

00:41:31.392 --> 00:41:32.600
Now I'm going to run the job.

00:41:37.990 --> 00:41:39.560
Again, we're appending.

00:41:39.560 --> 00:41:41.110
US-West is fine.

00:41:41.110 --> 00:41:44.010
We'll hit Run Job.

00:41:44.010 --> 00:41:44.740
All right.

00:41:44.740 --> 00:41:46.730
So it's going to run the job.

00:41:46.730 --> 00:41:48.610
I've easily
standardized the data.

00:41:48.610 --> 00:41:51.280
And then I quickly
use that target schema

00:41:51.280 --> 00:41:53.500
that we loaded into
the transformer grid

00:41:53.500 --> 00:41:58.900
to get my source data ready
for publication into BigQuery.

00:41:58.900 --> 00:42:00.325
So that covers the second part.

00:42:00.325 --> 00:42:01.390
SEAN MA: Well, that
looked like fun.

00:42:01.390 --> 00:42:02.650
All you were doing was
just kind of clicking

00:42:02.650 --> 00:42:05.040
and it automatically
transformed everything for you.

00:42:05.040 --> 00:42:05.748
CINDY SOOD: Yeah.

00:42:05.748 --> 00:42:07.708
Kind of like a video game.

00:42:07.708 --> 00:42:08.500
SEAN MA: All right.

00:42:08.500 --> 00:42:11.430
So now we'll just wrap
up coming to the end.

00:42:14.120 --> 00:42:16.340
Very quickly, we'll
cover the topics--

00:42:16.340 --> 00:42:18.145
just takeaways from
what we just covered,

00:42:18.145 --> 00:42:20.270
and I'll talk a little
about some of the next steps

00:42:20.270 --> 00:42:22.853
that you might be interested in.

00:42:22.853 --> 00:42:25.270
So if we remember, we covered
four topics, four challenges

00:42:25.270 --> 00:42:26.560
that we're going after.

00:42:26.560 --> 00:42:30.520
First was nested data, how do
you expand and extract values

00:42:30.520 --> 00:42:31.750
from that nested data.

00:42:31.750 --> 00:42:33.238
And so we saw examples of that.

00:42:33.238 --> 00:42:35.530
Following that, when you're
dealing with data patterns,

00:42:35.530 --> 00:42:38.200
take advantage of the
column profile details,

00:42:38.200 --> 00:42:40.090
and then use the
suggested transforms

00:42:40.090 --> 00:42:41.690
to actually fix those formats.

00:42:41.690 --> 00:42:44.145
You don't have to write
everything from scratch.

00:42:44.145 --> 00:42:45.520
When it comes to
standardization,

00:42:45.520 --> 00:42:48.280
use multiple algorithms, build
that group as much as possible,

00:42:48.280 --> 00:42:49.900
so you get the widest set.

00:42:49.900 --> 00:42:52.105
And the last one to note
is, when it comes down

00:42:52.105 --> 00:42:54.230
to publishing, before you
do that, quickly validate

00:42:54.230 --> 00:42:56.920
it, make sure that your data
actually matches your target

00:42:56.920 --> 00:43:00.070
and hopefully it can solve
a lot of the minor details

00:43:00.070 --> 00:43:03.250
that you need in order
to make it successful.

00:43:03.250 --> 00:43:05.600
These were just a few of
the powerful techniques

00:43:05.600 --> 00:43:07.558
and transformations that
are available in Cloud

00:43:07.558 --> 00:43:10.120
Dataprep that help you gain
insight inside your data

00:43:10.120 --> 00:43:13.380
all through a simple
easy to use interface.

00:43:13.380 --> 00:43:15.130
We didn't have a chance
to talk about some

00:43:15.130 --> 00:43:17.230
of the sharing and
automation capabilities that

00:43:17.230 --> 00:43:19.510
are a part of Dataprep,
so you can build out

00:43:19.510 --> 00:43:21.100
and maintain your pipeline.

00:43:21.100 --> 00:43:24.300
But if you're interested
in learning more,

00:43:24.300 --> 00:43:25.608
we do have a booth here.

00:43:25.608 --> 00:43:27.900
It's between the server-less,
the analytics and machine

00:43:27.900 --> 00:43:29.520
learning zones,
where you can ask

00:43:29.520 --> 00:43:32.310
Dataprep experts any
questions that you may have,

00:43:32.310 --> 00:43:34.890
as well you can sign up
for our training workshop.

00:43:34.890 --> 00:43:36.360
We do these things in person.

00:43:36.360 --> 00:43:38.670
And then lastly, if you
want more examples, things

00:43:38.670 --> 00:43:40.712
that you've seen outside
here, please take a look

00:43:40.712 --> 00:43:43.050
at the Dataprep community.

00:43:43.050 --> 00:43:45.862
And one last part, it's
for the folks who actually

00:43:45.862 --> 00:43:47.820
haven't tried Cloud
Dataprep and were just here

00:43:47.820 --> 00:43:50.815
just as an intro, you can try it
today, just go to your console

00:43:50.815 --> 00:43:52.690
and just launch Dataprep
directly from there.

00:43:52.690 --> 00:43:55.400
It's in the big data section.

00:43:55.400 --> 00:43:57.970
And with that,
thank you very much.

